#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "11f5bedf-cfc8-5ace-b080-5ec30b406a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7436" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/0ae3d46a511cfe74b489eec33e6f6fec0adcb5be7d955a1880f979963b0e01f6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "29cfe1ff-ca64-51f9-8da1-5d0f798711f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7437" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-64x \\\\ 4p & =-64 \\\\ p & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "07678ad9-a16f-5fd2-8e39-85376aa9112e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7442" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3afa95b7-02e2-5ada-bc15-1419124e03ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285792} {:answer/id #uuid "5e742002-4a38-5ead-a25e-c6f22714768d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285793} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f5d15d-e732-5574-932f-1825df384cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285794} {:answer/id #uuid "f8434fc9-b25f-587e-a7fe-02ef17092fd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285795} {:answer/id #uuid "711ac830-b9b2-50d0-b382-c8924c3f09bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3afa95b7-02e2-5ada-bc15-1419124e03ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285792} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8795698-e693-5154-b430-5eaeb64341d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "827bc944-9468-5f1f-aaa1-d206a7a58e66" :question/math-academy-id "q-106412" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/c42f5aac98d31b7a66a60eebae34b5e83cb62f3ba4801595a81dd9bb57706384.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,-8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97bfb9ef-3b21-59a5-b2de-dbce50578c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285799} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf26b1b-c1e2-5d0b-9c3a-0d41490094fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285800} {:answer/id #uuid "a370caac-e18e-5e57-8775-60e2285abcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285801} {:answer/id #uuid "474b91e9-76ab-55bc-bf3e-6da4e94fe85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285802} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4f9285-417b-56b7-9d67-fdcf50a6ede0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3t,\\,\\,y=6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a370caac-e18e-5e57-8775-60e2285abcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285801} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6db4438-92a4-525c-881f-a8f8e59c4c86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "200bd8e0-2a01-528c-8a4d-6d1ad4880558" :question/math-academy-id "q-106420" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=12x?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =12x \\\\ 4p & =12 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecf92462-3fc8-5bad-af56-fa3683facda0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302005} {:answer/id #uuid "134e073f-3d1b-5800-bae6-e53fd31a6a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "134e073f-3d1b-5800-bae6-e53fd31a6a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302006} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c43cf81-741b-5dbd-ac2f-299df4af5bc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f32889c4-e261-5d80-b6d8-340aaee1bedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f32889c4-e261-5d80-b6d8-340aaee1bedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302008} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e61122e-9c71-514c-9b53-fabfbed77748" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dccd6f7f-a149-5a6a-8939-acec8e542598" :question/math-academy-id "q-274755" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=24x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =24x \\\\ 4p & =24 \\\\ p & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(6){t}^{2},\\,y=2(6)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=6{t}^{2},\\,y=12t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66bb59e0-0b5b-5164-bd40-f0fee88b366a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66bb59e0-0b5b-5164-bd40-f0fee88b366a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302011} :answer-field/id #uuid "f65c54d0-936e-522a-bdb8-7755c98b8b51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f50a61-b52e-595f-a829-f380f3897629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5f50a61-b52e-595f-a829-f380f3897629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302013} :answer-field/id #uuid "62da1243-ce30-514a-804d-f1f4c6c1156a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8e7fd54-cc70-5931-a4e3-d16151002a6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-280209" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/79609f38fef6121500dfc553137a6dd3af3a85ecd838a098c452748ab51ea13d.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-6,6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-6,6),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=6$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6=2pt \\\\ 6=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 6 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{3}{2})t,\\,y=(\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc61aab1-4418-5b27-9b42-a81f50305e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302016} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea81d68-8b7a-50c8-8bed-a514a0d13eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ea81d68-8b7a-50c8-8bed-a514a0d13eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302017} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebbee884-d5e2-5168-8fea-d5073dc718ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75426c62-467a-5afd-8392-9af2ee415a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75426c62-467a-5afd-8392-9af2ee415a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302019} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4870d69-93b4-5290-a321-705f47bb4bcc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c845806-ad5b-5436-a3d5-7e58cbbec065" :question/math-academy-id "q-280210" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/704181219623dc7269e7bcbb4de75c4181a5be4db3a097eb3d9da141dc64a403.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(10,5).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(10,5),$ we can substitute $x=10$ and $y=5$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}10=2pt \\\\ 5=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2pt \\\\ 5 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =p{(\\frac{5}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{25}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{25}{p} \\\\ p & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(5)t,\\,y=(5){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=10t,\\,y=5{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4d193e-1ad7-5fcf-ac16-6bb379c93fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d4d193e-1ad7-5fcf-ac16-6bb379c93fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302022} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe55af9-51c1-5991-9369-d12d55790a91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e8a7e7-06b5-599f-b6d8-622108988027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e8a7e7-06b5-599f-b6d8-622108988027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302024} :answer-field/id #uuid "fefe99a7-0ff1-514f-9f69-963e98783e52" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe751547-4c45-5462-a7a1-024423d932ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-280226" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=32x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =32x \\\\ 4p & =32 \\\\ p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(8){t}^{2},\\,y=2(8)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=8{t}^{2},\\,y=16t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bed0c2b0-24f9-5ccd-aba1-0e03715b8e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285832} {:answer/id #uuid "75c2fe08-1bcf-5695-a7cc-2aca55b78d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed0c2b0-24f9-5ccd-aba1-0e03715b8e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285832} :answer-field/id #uuid "9292f618-20fc-5f5b-b651-d46d9c09e736" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285831} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8960adf9-f3a6-585b-b81a-c530c948f3dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8960adf9-f3a6-585b-b81a-c530c948f3dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285835} :answer-field/id #uuid "146d0acb-1c7a-5699-899c-0a0fafd6cc94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "864c98fc-1abc-5908-a417-e5cb64cfdbb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-280233" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/d8e4aa88acf2a0d69d4c4208dc4951a33cc92e59e177395ec74be3ae0f9a8c7f.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the parabola shown above. Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-4$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{4}{5}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{4}{5})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{8}{5}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b64a9142-e4a9-5c18-9064-3ee9de13aa48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b64a9142-e4a9-5c18-9064-3ee9de13aa48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302027} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d7828e-a623-5953-b920-7d10d61af143" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d77dd31d-335e-5a60-b88d-a30ad6826051" :question/math-academy-id "q-280337" :question/problem "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=4{t}^{2},\\,y=6t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{4},\\,t=\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=9x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f803e75-9b26-5e64-a1d6-7b797e1d6f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f803e75-9b26-5e64-a1d6-7b797e1d6f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292942} :answer-field/id #uuid "e99c9df6-f2cc-5ed9-a171-8462a5376590" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fe4c9ac-c3be-5ca2-8785-0a4e82bd680c" :question/math-academy-id "q-280361" :question/problem "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=-2t,\\,y=-\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${x}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{3y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{3y}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =-\\frac{3y}{4} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-3y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "729bfb4b-e122-5a02-a844-4f165e3c7b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302030} {:answer/id #uuid "74f12e0f-e8b2-5911-bd0e-50b818fa3cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302031} {:answer/id #uuid "d2aaeaa2-a18b-5e77-a272-02c8cb810a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302032} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8d3bf6-e090-5df5-a794-98f8ba9231b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302033} {:answer/id #uuid "c1014d46-72b2-5674-8f29-021edef19c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-6t,\\,\\,y=6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74f12e0f-e8b2-5911-bd0e-50b818fa3cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302031} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0af0c52-2abe-561e-947c-ce887d05910c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8369bdd-8409-5776-b1cd-08895b84e231" :question/math-academy-id "q-84673" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/237e807e6a36b3a6b5ac92fc3fa93d046761170ac2558511020ce382f70fd3c0.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(6,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,-6),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=-6$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6=2pt \\\\ -6=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ 3 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -6 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -6 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-3t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e88d0df-06b6-5434-8ee7-e41bd662943d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-7t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285849} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd72ae6-89a1-57ef-890b-9a8b14f83058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-7{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285850} {:answer/id #uuid "19eb8185-2670-53bd-a4a1-8cf6bfc406b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285851} {:answer/id #uuid "aee65ba4-52fa-5592-9962-0538b650591b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7t,\\,\\,y=-14{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285852} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce7f774-d75a-59d6-b1aa-0d0940d8bbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19eb8185-2670-53bd-a4a1-8cf6bfc406b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285851} :answer-field/id #uuid "4728aa8d-ba4c-59c6-bb37-aca322427c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51659ed2-0d6d-5995-abed-0cbe41395da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-84675" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-7,-7)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-7,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-7$ and $y=-7$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-7=2pt \\\\ -7=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{7}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =p{(-\\frac{7}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -7 & =p\\cdot \\frac{49}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -7 & =\\frac{49}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-7 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{7}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{7}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcb94400-dd57-5b09-8e7f-910b97699f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302037} {:answer/id #uuid "e9648099-e850-5fbc-b47f-bca7faaa16ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{16}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302038} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0e034d-0031-5fce-9eb1-12c0031be31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=16t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302039} {:answer/id #uuid "d7138c93-8e56-5813-b4aa-792350b0208d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302040} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f0b71c-e4bd-552b-aeea-14c4fdf779ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{8}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcb94400-dd57-5b09-8e7f-910b97699f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302037} :answer-field/id #uuid "d28120e2-a08f-5f8b-95af-21c31a5a869b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56c1507b-07af-5ef5-b091-f6c50ac855a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-84719" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039122/assets/c8ec60cf31b66610fd9ddc8fc3fb8c30e8a27d2de41b6ecdeb358afa60588cf4.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(8,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,2),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=2$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(\\frac{1}{8}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(\\frac{1}{8})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99b15ed6-6bda-5044-8b7c-fc26fe4f54f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = \\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087205} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e88bbc-8c36-5fb5-b24d-b3ae8ba39029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087206} {:answer/id #uuid "c849c718-e8ac-5350-8540-b1edf64aa151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = \\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087207} {:answer/id #uuid "17cfed83-a330-5fce-8fa3-f03773ac9b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087208} {:answer/id #uuid "3c76ab66-c8bf-5950-9907-615e4779d41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17cfed83-a330-5fce-8fa3-f03773ac9b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087208} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e8ed5a8-cc21-5f75-9f4a-3cd3fa9cf18e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50547e51-0957-5746-a2ba-be9442cc3fec" :question/math-academy-id "q-84731" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4x,\\,t=2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=4x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c142ead1-0a6d-5542-a04c-8db3b4e8f77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302044} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bc8864-4854-50e0-ab04-ffbb4637ea32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302045} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7117b4-00c1-5b6c-a267-9390c1d9fcee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302046} {:answer/id #uuid "e56c4328-3af9-5f8e-bb91-f0c63419aab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302047} {:answer/id #uuid "8fade18f-079e-5c1d-93c2-05e6ba815a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5bc8864-4854-50e0-ab04-ffbb4637ea32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302045} :answer-field/id #uuid "875b6ea3-3843-55d2-9c29-32772ae7ae09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d723da4-6d35-5b58-905a-5236b3db4ebf" :question/math-academy-id "q-84734" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-2{t}^{2},\\,y=-4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-8x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4847fced-caaf-5469-bd8e-59974b22bc1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302051} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcaa8b5-b930-5f37-a0c2-df9d7b3f6afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302052} {:answer/id #uuid "80dfda35-dc05-5f88-9248-0677f6cb8a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302053} {:answer/id #uuid "f81c1289-421c-5521-bf88-bea86b19ec41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302054} {:answer/id #uuid "8be5a62e-8442-5404-b771-50ca6c0271a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{8}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f81c1289-421c-5521-bf88-bea86b19ec41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302054} :answer-field/id #uuid "49196a48-8df7-5859-a98f-3671eb0a600d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2457959a-83fb-599c-9b5d-d9f0379d3f2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-84736" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2}{3}y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=6y.$"}]}
